  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAs of 239 AM EDT...High pressure will continue to build in from\nthe Great Lakes Region and the Ohio Valley today with fair, \ncool and dry weather. Humidity levels will be very comfortable \ntoday and Friday with sfc dewpoints in the mid 40s to mid 50s \nacross eastern NY and western New England. After patchy morning \nmist/fog burns off, max temps will run about 5 degrees below \nnormal for late July with 60s to lower 70s over the higher \nterrain and mid 70s to around 80F in the valleys. Ideal \nradiational cooling is expected tonight and we went closer to \nthe cooler MAVMOS guidance values with lows in the mid 40s to \nlower 50s. Some patchy radiative mist/fog is possible in the \nvalley areas Fri morning. The strong subsidence from the 1025 \nhPa sfc anticyclone moving in over eastern NY and western New \nEngland Fri afternoon will yield lots of sunshine and pleasant \nconditions with max temps near to slightly above normal with \nhighs in the 70s to lower 80s. The high slowly retreats eastward\nFri night for yet another cool night, where we blended the NBM \nand MAV guidance for lows in the lower to mid 50s with some \nupper 40s in the southwest Adirondacks and southern Greens.\n\nTemps trend slightly above normal on the weekend with some weak\nridging near the Northeast/New England Coast. The return \nsouth/southwest flow from the sfc high east of New England will \nallow max temps to be generally in the lower to mid 80s in the \nmajor valleys and 70s to around 80F over the hills and mtns. \nLows will be milder in the 50s to lower 60s both mornings. \nDewpoints rise into the mid 50s to lower 60s, as humidity \nlevels will be moderate at best, and heat indices are not \nexpected to be an issue. Overall...a great stretch of late July \nweather for today through the weekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA frontal boundary and stronger short-wave trough approaches\nfrom the Great Lakes Region Mon into Tue. Chances of showers and\na few thunderstorms increase early in the week. At this time,\nthe coverage and amount of instability for strong t-storms is \nuncertain on Mon. The mid and upper level trough moves NY and \nNew England Tue into Wed with widespread showers and some \nthunderstorms possible. The latest NAEFS indicates low and mid \nlevel heights may fall 2-3 STDEVs below normal over the \nNortheast. The medium range guidance, ensembles and NBM all \nsupport the highest probabilities of a widespread rainfall Tue-\nWed with PoPs in the 55-75% range. The latest 01Z/23 NBM \nindicates a 45-60% chance >0.50\" rainfall across eastern NY and\nwestern New England 12Z Tue to 12Z Wed from the sfc low and its\nassociated frontal boundaries. Temp trend slightly above normal\nMon to below normal by Wed based on the latest WPC forecast in \nDays 4 to 7 of the forecast for eastern NY and western New \nEngland.",
